124,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,47822,16,17,92,284,1124,1242,15159,74,28,15,92,47822,16,17,92,1124,6863,316,90,16,17,15170,74,92,19788,26888,90,17,5410,47822,16,17,12646,92,19788,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,61,74,198,59,2533,1654,1184,311,8253,892,3793,304,419,14461,525,24438,13,362,4647,374,24438,421,2176,1124,11520,26888,90,17,11035,8,323,1124,11520,26888,58,18,15370,18,11035,8,525,9226,311,7546,13430,429,1102,304,24438,5109,13,44763,11,1124,1188,59,26888,90,17,5410,47822,16,17,12646,11035,8,374,24438,421,17767,16,17,12646,57758,374,1496,11,323,1124,1188,59,26888,58,18,15370,18,5410,61,74,57758,374,24438,421,17767,74,57758,374,264,5248,315,220,18,13,15277,11,17767,74,57758,1969,26553,2176,4682,25,17767,74,57758,1969,387,264,5248,315,220,18,323,17767,16,17,12646,57758,1969,387,1496,382,10061,594,1140,279,3204,2750,315,17767,74,59,1648,1406,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of\n\\[(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}.\\]\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we will use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine which terms in this expansion are rational. A term is rational if both \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[3]{3}\\) are raised to integer powers that result in rational numbers. Specifically, \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) is rational if \\(12-k\\) is even, and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) is rational if \\(k\\) is a multiple of 3. Therefore, \\(k\\) must satisfy both conditions: \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 and \\(12-k\\) must be even.\n\nLet's list the possible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b99c13840700fa36b3790f2d75c57baa9a7c7c2e73b3513a0864fb6724b12421"},"seconds":45.41265273094177,"seed":695817121362541700,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we will use the binomial theorem. According to the binomial theorem, the expansion is given by\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k.\n\\]\n\nA term in the expansion will be rational if both \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) are rational numbers. This means that \\(12-k\\) must be even (so that \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) is a power of 2) and \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 (so that \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) is a power of 3). We now list the values of \\(k\\) that satisfy both conditions:\n\n- \\(k = 0\\): \\(12-0 = 12\\) (even) and \\(0\\) is a multiple of 3.\n- \\(k = 3\\): \\(12-3 = 9\\) (odd) and \\(3\\) is a multiple of 3.\n- \\(k = 6\\): \\(12-6 = 6\\) (even) and \\(6\\) is a multiple of 3.\n- \\(k = 9\\): \\(12-9 = 3\\) (odd) and \\(9\\) is a multiple of 3.\n- \\(k = 12\\): \\(12-12 = 0\\) (even) and \\(12\\) is a multiple of 3.\n\nSo, the values of \\(k\\) that give rational terms are \\(k = 0, 6, 12\\). We now calculate these terms:\n\n1. For \\(k = 0\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot 2^6 \\cdot 1 = 64.\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(k = 6\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^{6} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = \\binom{12}{6} \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 66528.\n\\]\n\n3. For \\(k = 12\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^{0} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 81.\n\\]\n\nThe sum of all the rational terms is:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\) is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{66673}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\), we will use the binomial theorem. According to the binomial theorem, the expansion is given by\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = \\sum_{k=0}^{12} \\binom{12}{k} (\\sqrt{2})^{12-k} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^k.\n\\]\n\nA term in the expansion will be rational if both \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) and \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) are rational numbers. This means that \\(12-k\\) must be even (so that \\((\\sqrt{2})^{12-k}\\) is a power of 2) and \\(k\\) must be a multiple of 3 (so that \\((\\sqrt[3]{3})^k\\) is a power of 3). We now list the values of \\(k\\) that satisfy both conditions:\n\n- \\(k = 0\\): \\(12-0 = 12\\) (even) and \\(0\\) is a multiple of 3.\n- \\(k = 3\\): \\(12-3 = 9\\) (odd) and \\(3\\) is a multiple of 3.\n- \\(k = 6\\): \\(12-6 = 6\\) (even) and \\(6\\) is a multiple of 3.\n- \\(k = 9\\): \\(12-9 = 3\\) (odd) and \\(9\\) is a multiple of 3.\n- \\(k = 12\\): \\(12-12 = 0\\) (even) and \\(12\\) is a multiple of 3.\n\nSo, the values of \\(k\\) that give rational terms are \\(k = 0, 6, 12\\). We now calculate these terms:\n\n1. For \\(k = 0\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{0} (\\sqrt{2})^{12} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^0 = 1 \\cdot 2^6 \\cdot 1 = 64.\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(k = 6\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{6} (\\sqrt{2})^{6} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^6 = \\binom{12}{6} \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 924 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 9 = 66528.\n\\]\n\n3. For \\(k = 12\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{12} (\\sqrt{2})^{0} (\\sqrt[3]{3})^{12} = 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 3^4 = 81.\n\\]\n\nThe sum of all the rational terms is:\n\\[\n64 + 66528 + 81 = 66673.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all the rational terms in the expansion of \\((\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt[3]{3})^{12}\\) is \\(\\boxed{66673}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:445"}
